  "output": "I have enough verified code to give you an accurate analysis. Here's what I found:\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis — HR Policy Bot\n\n### 1. New DB Connection Per Operation (High Impact)\n\n`src/data/db.py` opens a fresh `aiosqlite.connect()` for **every single method call**. Since a full query pipeline calls `save_query` → `save_agent_response` (×N) → `save_orchestrator_response`, that's 3–10 connection open/close cycles in sequence.\n\n**Current pattern (`db.py:92`, `111`, `129`, `152`, `177`...):**\n```python\nasync def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:  # new conn every call\n        await db.execute(...)\n        await db.commit()\n\nasync def get_agent_responses(self, query_id: str) -> list[AgentResponseRecord]:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:  # another new conn\n        ...\n```\n\n**Fix — add a persistent connection with WAL mode:**\n```python\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path: Path = _DB_PATH) -> None:\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._db: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n\n    async def initialize(self) -> None:\n        self._db = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n        self._db.row_factory = aiosqlite.Row\n        await self._db.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n        await self._db.executescript(_SCHEMA)\n        await self._db.commit()\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._db:\n            await self._db.close()\n```\nThen replace every `async with aiosqlite.connect(...)` with `async with self._db.execute(...)`. Register `close()` in FastAPI's `lifespan` handler.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `_make_model()` Called on Every LLM Request (High Impact)\n\n`src/llm/client.py:40–50` creates a new `genai.GenerativeModel` object on every `chat()`, `stream_chat()`, and `chat_parsed()` call. The model object can be cached — only `json_output` mode differs.\n\n**Current (`client.py:70–95`):**\n```python\nasync def chat(self, ...) -> str:\n    m = self._make_model(model)           # new object every call\n    ...\n\nasync def stream_chat(self, ...) -> AsyncGenerator[str, None]:\n    m = self._make_model(model)           # new object every call\n    ...\n\nasync def chat_parsed(self, ...) -> dict:\n    m = self._make_model(model, json_output=True)  # new object every call\n    ...\n```\n\n**Fix — cache both variants:**\n```python\nclass GeminiClient:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        ...\n        self._model_text = self._make_model()\n        self._model_json = self._make_model(json_output=True)\n\n    async def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> str:\n        m = self._model_text if model is None else self._make_model(model)\n        ...\n\n    async def chat_parsed(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> dict:\n        m = self._model_json if model is None else self._make_model(model, json_output=True)\n        ...\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Policy Registry Parsed from Disk 8× at Startup\n\n`src/agents/specialist.py:72–75` reads and parses `policy_registry.json` once per specialist. With 8 specialists all initialized at startup, that's 8 file opens + JSON parses of the same file.\n\n**Current:**\n```python\ndef _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n    with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:      # disk I/O × 8\n        registry = json.load(f)          # JSON parse × 8\n    return [r for r in registry if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n**Fix — module-level cache:**\n```python\n_REGISTRY_CACHE: list[dict] | None = None\n\ndef _get_registry() -> list[dict]:\n    global _REGISTRY_CACHE\n    if _REGISTRY_CACHE is None:\n        with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n            _REGISTRY_CACHE = json.load(f)\n    return _REGISTRY_CACHE\n\nclass SpecialistAgent(GenericAgent):\n    def _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n        return [r for r in _get_registry() if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. `save_query` Called Twice Per Query (Redundant Write)\n\n`src/api/websocket.py:162,182` — `save_query` is called once at the start with an empty `agents_invoked` list, then again after completion to update it. Both calls open separate DB connections and write the same primary key twice.\n\n**Current (`websocket.py:157–182`):**\n```python\nquery_record = QueryRecord(query_id=query_id, query_text=query_text, agents_invoked=[])\nawait db_manager.save_query(query_record)     # write 1 — incomplete data\n\n# ... run orchestrator ...\n\nquery_record.agents_invoked = response.agents_consulted\nquery_record.overall_confidence = response.overall_confidence\nawait db_manager.save_query(query_record)     # write 2 — overwrite same row\n```\n\n**Fix — write once at completion, or use a targeted UPDATE:**\n```python\n# Remove the first save_query call.\n# After orchestration completes, do a single insert:\nquery_record = QueryRecord(\n    query_id=query_id,\n    query_text=query_text,\n    agents_invoked=response.agents_consulted,\n    overall_confidence=response.overall_confidence,\n    processing_time_ms=int(processing_time),\n)\nawait db_manager.save_query(query_record)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. No HTTP Fetch Caching Hit for Repeated URLs Across Specialists\n\nThe `FetchEngine` has a cache, but each specialist independently fetches its instruments. If two specialists share a URL (e.g., a Values and Ethics document referenced by both `governance` and `equity`), the second fetch hits the cache — but the fetch still opens a DB connection. More importantly, there is no in-process LRU to avoid the SQLite round-trip for recently-fetched hot pages.\n\n**Fix — add a small in-memory LRU in front of the SQLite cache:**\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nclass FetchEngine:\n    def __init__(self, ...):\n        ...\n        self._hot_cache: dict[str, str] = {}   # URL → text, bounded\n\n    async def fetch(self, url: str) -> str:\n        url = self._normalize_url(url)\n        if url in self._hot_cache:\n            return self._hot_cache[url]\n        async with self._global_semaphore:\n            result = await self._fetch_with_concurrency(url)\n        if len(self._hot_cache) < 128:          # cap size\n            self._hot_cache[url] = result\n        return result\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. `broadcast_error` Sends to All Clients — Logic Bug\n\n`src/api/websocket.py:55–62` — an exception in one query's WebSocket loop triggers `broadcast_error`, which iterates over **all** active connections and sends the error to unrelated users. This is almost certainly unintentional.\n\n**Current:**\n```python\nasync def broadcast_error(self, message: str, query_id: str | None = None) -> None:\n    for qid in list(self._connections.keys()):   # sends to ALL sessions\n        await self._connections[qid].send_json(error.model_dump())\n```\n\n**Fix — send only to the relevant query_id:**\n```python\nasync def send_error(self, message: str, query_id: str) -> None:\n    error = WSError(query_id=query_id, message=message)\n    await self.send(query_id, error.model_dump())\n```\nUpdate the caller at `websocket.py:122`:\n```python\nawait ws_manager.send_error(str(e), query_id)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. `_domain_last_request` Dict Grows Unbounded\n\n`src/fetch/engine.py:46` uses a `defaultdict(float)` to track per-domain request times. Domains are never evicted, so after fetching from many domains across a long-running server lifetime, this dict grows indefinitely.\n\n**Fix — use a bounded `OrderedDict` or cap eviction:**\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass FetchEngine:\n    _MAX_DOMAIN_ENTRIES = 500\n\n    def __init__(self, ...):\n        self._domain_last_request: OrderedDict[str, float] = OrderedDict()\n\n    def _record_domain_request(self, domain: str) -> None:\n        self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.monotonic()\n        self._domain_last_request.move_to_end(domain)\n        if len(self._domain_last_request) > self._MAX_DOMAIN_ENTRIES:\n            self._domain_last_request.popitem(last=False)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| # | Issue | File | Severity | Effort |\n|---|-------|------|----------|--------|\n| 1 | New SQLite connection per DB method | `db.py:82–260` | High | Medium |\n| 2 | `GenerativeModel` recreated every LLM call | `client.py:40–95` | High | Low |\n| 3 | Registry JSON parsed 8× at startup | `specialist.py:72–75` | Medium | Trivial |\n| 4 | `save_query` called twice per query | `websocket.py:162,182` | Medium | Low |\n| 5 | No in-process hot cache for HTTP fetches | `engine.py:61` | Medium | Low |\n| 6 | `broadcast_error` sends to all sessions | `websocket.py:55–62` | Logic bug | Trivial |\n| 7 | `_domain_last_request` unbounded growth | `engine.py:46` | Low | Low |\n\nItems 2, 3, and 6 are trivial one-liners. Items 1 and 4 together will have the most visible latency impact since every query currently opens 5–10+ SQLite connections serially.\n",
